# examples/cloginrc — a RANCID .cloginrc read as data (docs/CREDENTIAL-CSV.md
# for the file backends): karvi takes the add user and add password lines,
# passes over the other RANCID statements it knows (autoenable, method, ...)
# with a warning naming the line, and refuses any other; it never runs the
# file. Copy it to ~/.cloginrc and chmod 600, or declare it
# as [credential-backend.rancid] in the configuration (examples/config.toml).
# A # starts a comment; a continuation line (a trailing backslash) is refused.
#
# add user PATTERN {USERNAME}            the login name for the devices PATTERN matches
# add password PATTERN {PASSWORD} {ENABLE}  the login and the enable password
# The passwords here are placeholders; the first matching line wins.

add user edge-* {svc.edge}
add password edge-* {edge-placeholder} {enable-placeholder}

add user core-* {svc.core}
add password core-* {core-placeholder} {enable-placeholder}

# the rest: the operator's own name is not assumed by .cloginrc, so name one
add user * {svc.default}
add password * {default-placeholder}
